# tests/T06.bd - LENS 3 / T06.
#
# QUESTION: does try/catch intercept the "[SYSTEM] CPU overloaded. All
# processes terminated." kill, or is that kill an unconditional
# engine-level termination no in-script handler can ever see?
#
# WHY THIS RUNS BEFORE EVERY OTHER KILL-BASED TEST: every crash location
# in this investigation so far was INFERRED from whichever debug
# checkpoint printed last - an approach field_notes.txt itself flags as
# resting on unverified terminal-flush-on-death semantics. If the kill
# is catchable, every future test can log its own exact death point, the
# meter at death, and then KEEP RUNNING: direct measurement replacing
# inference. That payoff is out of all proportion to the ~20s this costs.
#
# EXPECTED (the hypothesis this is trying to kill): NOT catchable. The
# banner says "All processes terminated" and it kills OTHER scripts too,
# which reads as an external process stop, not a language-level error.
#
# THE BURST IS THE EXPERIMENT. Phases 2 and 4 are deliberately UNPACED:
# burst_n iterations, ~2x that many consecutive ticks, roughly 8x the
# ~50-tick lethal dose established by test_cpu_decay.bd's 50-line
# arithmetic burst. EVERY other line in this file is paced (never more
# than 3 executed lines between sleeps) so that nothing except the two
# bursts can possibly be the thing that dies.
#
# CPU TIER ASSUMPTION: base tier, 10 ticks/sec - consistent with every
# reading on record (idle get_cpu() sits at exactly 10.0 for a
# sleep(1)+log() cadence; the live meter moves in multiples of 10). If
# this save has upgraded since, scale burst_n below or the burst may no
# longer be lethal and the run will merely imitate the "not lethal"
# outcome: Mk.II 400, Mk.III 800, Mk.IV 1600, Mk.V 3200.
#
# PHASE ORDER IS THE DESIGN. Phases are sequenced so the riskiest
# statements always come LAST, after everything they could destroy has
# already printed:
#   P1 CONTROL  raise() inside try/catch. Proves try/catch actually
#               works in THIS script, THIS session, and that a catch
#               block can log. Without it, a silent P2 is meaningless -
#               "not catchable" and "try/catch mis-typed here" look
#               identical. Touches no error-object fields: zero risk.
#   P2 FLAT     the unpaced burst at top level inside try. The answer.
#               The handler leads with sleep(3) ON PURPOSE: if it runs
#               at all it runs with the meter pinned at the ceiling, and
#               an unpaced statement there could be killed before it
#               printed anything - which would masquerade as "not
#               catchable". sleep() is 0 ticks and yields immediately.
#   P3 RESUMED  a top-level line AFTER the try/catch, so it does not
#               depend on the handler. Answers the follow-up that
#               actually decides methodology: does execution CONTINUE
#               past a caught overload? Its k= value is a free direct
#               measurement of how many iterations ran before the kill.
#   P4 NESTED   the same burst one call deep (burn()), because the docs
#               claim try/catch "works across nested function calls" and
#               any real self-reporting test must wrap work that lives
#               in functions. Free: only reachable if P2 was caught.
#               ALL risky error-object reads live here (e.message/e.line
#               are documented but never once exercised in this project,
#               and an error raised inside a catch block cannot itself
#               be caught) - so if reading them fails, nothing of value
#               is downstream of the failure.
#
# HOW TO READ THE OUTPUT:
#   "P1 CONTROL caught=1", then output STOPS after "P2 flat burst
#       starting NOW", banner, nothing else   -> NOT CATCHABLE. The kill
#       is external to the language. Expected result. No follow-up.
#   "P2 FLAT CAUGHT=1" prints                 -> CATCHABLE. Methodology
#       unlock. Report P3's k= and P4's e.message/e.line VERBATIM.
#   "P3 resumed caught=0" at roughly +45s     -> BURST NOT LETHAL. ~400
#       unpaced ticks killed nothing, contradicting test_cpu_decay.bd.
#       Stop, skip the repeats, re-run the burst-size bisection.
#   no "P1 CONTROL caught=1" line at all      -> TEST VOID. try/catch
#       did not behave as documented; report raw output.
#   nothing after the P3 line                 -> the handler could not
#       execute even one statement at ceiling load. Noteworthy, but it
#       does NOT affect the P2 answer, which already printed.
#
# HOW TO RUN (no parameters):
#     T06
#
# REPEATS: 3 runs, ~20-30s idle between them, ps/jobs confirmed empty.
# If a run's "T06 start cpu=" reads above ~20, wait longer before the
# next one. Paste ALL output verbatim and say explicitly WHETHER the
# overload banner appeared and WHERE, relative to the T06 lines. Three
# runs because one silent run cannot separate "not catchable" from "the
# handler was killed before it could print"; three identical runs plus
# the P1 control is the cheapest way to close that gap.

func burn(n):
    var i = 0
    while i < n:
        i = i + 1
    return i

var burst_n = 200
var t0 = get_unix_time()
log("T06 start cpu=" + str(get_cpu()) + " burst_n=" + str(burst_n))
sleep(2)

var ctl = 0
try:
    raise("T06 control probe")
catch e1:
    ctl = 1
    log("T06 P1 CONTROL caught=1 - try/catch works here, a catch block can log")
sleep(2)
log("T06 P1 done ctl=" + str(ctl) + " t=+" + str(get_unix_time() - t0) + "s cpu=" + str(get_cpu()))
sleep(3)

var caught = 0
var k = 0
log("T06 P2 flat burst starting NOW - " + str(burst_n) + " unpaced iterations, cpu=" + str(get_cpu()))
sleep(3)
try:
    while k < burst_n:
        k = k + 1
catch e2:
    sleep(3)
    caught = 1
    log("T06 P2 FLAT CAUGHT=1 t=+" + str(get_unix_time() - t0) + "s cpu=" + str(get_cpu()))
sleep(3)
log("T06 P3 resumed caught=" + str(caught) + " k=" + str(k) + " t=+" + str(get_unix_time() - t0) + "s cpu=" + str(get_cpu()))
sleep(3)

var cdeath = 0
var r = 0
log("T06 P4 nested burst starting NOW - same burst inside burn(), cpu=" + str(get_cpu()))
sleep(3)
try:
    r = burn(burst_n)
catch e3:
    cdeath = get_cpu()
    sleep(3)
    log("T06 P4 NESTED CAUGHT=1 cpu_at_handler_entry=" + str(cdeath) + " t=+" + str(get_unix_time() - t0) + "s")
    sleep(1)
    log("T06 P4 e.message=" + str(e3.message))
    sleep(1)
    log("T06 P4 e.line=" + str(e3.line))
sleep(2)
log("T06 end r=" + str(r) + " caught=" + str(caught) + " t=+" + str(get_unix_time() - t0) + "s cpu=" + str(get_cpu()))
